WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.402
KitCycle explores how student clubs could avoid lending an incomplete kit. This is an edited

00:00:05.402 --> 00:00:10.805
walkthrough of actual app captures, using invented data and synthetic narration. It is a

00:00:10.805 --> 00:00:16.207
prototype, not a real lending service or a user study.

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:22.964
The shelf starts with two available kits and one repair hold. An invented club can request

00:00:22.964 --> 00:00:28.721
Circuit starter. That request does not reserve it. A volunteer must review and confirm

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:34.478
approval. The same kit cannot be approved for two active requests.

00:00:35.478 --> 00:00:40.658
Checkout requires every listed component. In the tested walkthrough, returning the kit with a

00:00:40.658 --> 00:00:45.838
missing USB cable and a repair note moves it into a repair hold. It cannot be lent again until

00:00:45.838 --> 00:00:51.019
an inspection note and confirmation clear the hold. These buttons simulate decisions; they

00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:56.199
cannot verify physical equipment.

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:02.654
The audit records requests, approval, checkout and return. The actual JSON download was checked

00:01:02.654 --> 00:01:08.109
against those four actions. Browser state persists across reloads. Other tabs refresh, and a

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:13.564
stale write is rejected. The audit is editable browser data, not a signed or tamper proof

00:01:13.564 --> 00:01:19.020
record.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.720
Ten core tests and a real local browser walkthrough passed, including corrupt storage handling

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:31.419
and a narrow screen. There are no accounts, role permissions or cloud synchronization. Use

00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:37.119
invented aliases only. Codex generated the new October thirty first implementation and

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:42.819
materials. The October twenty ninth concept plan is disclosed. Humans still need to review and

00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:48.519
understand the code before presenting it.
